\section*{Scriptural Date and Location}
\begingroup
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.03}
% Adjust column balance using the actual generated annotation widths, not smaller type.
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{0.15\linewidth}p{0.17\linewidth}p{0.23\linewidth}X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Textual unit / alternative} & \textbf{Citation} & \textbf{Location} & \textbf{Date}\\\midrule
Entrance basis & Ps. 37:39--40 (Vulgate 36) & Prayer amid affliction; exact composition place unresolved. & \chronodate{entrance}{\chronologyannotation{entrance}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{The composed antiphon's identified biblical basis is treated here as an adaptation, not as a verbatim extract or a historical event.}\\\midrule
Communion, Psalm alternative & Ps. 119:4--5 (Vulgate 118) & The prayer for directed ways; exact composition place unresolved. & \chronodate{communion-ps119}{\chronologyannotation{communion-ps119}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{The command is followed by a petition for faithful action. This is one alternative in the Communion slot.}\\\midrule
Responsorial Psalm & Ps. 145:2--3, 8--9, 17--18 (Vulgate 144); response 18a & Praise of the Lord's kingship; exact composition place unresolved. & \chronodate{responsorial-psalm}{\chronologyannotation{responsorial-psalm}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{The selected strophes join greatness, mercy and truthful invocation. A broad composition boundary does not date an individual psalm securely.}\\\midrule
First reading & Isaiah 55:6--9 & The prophetic summons to return, read within the chapter's covenant invitation. & \chronodate{first-reading}{\chronologyannotation{first-reading}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{Inherited book attribution and the chapter's return-from-Babylon horizon answer different questions. The latter is not the date of the book's composition.}\\\midrule
Gospel & Matthew 20:1--16a & Jesus' journey toward Jerusalem; a parable following Peter's question about reward. & \chronodate{gospel}{\chronologyannotation{gospel}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{The workers' day belongs to the parable, not to a dated employment event. The received composition dates remain disputed alternatives.}\\\midrule
Communion, John alternative & John 10:14 & The Shepherd discourse following the controversy over the man born blind. & \chronodate{communion-jn10}{\chronologyannotation{communion-jn10}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{Christ's knowledge of his sheep belongs to a discourse of self-offering; this alternative does not follow the Psalm alternative in a single enactment.}\\\midrule
Acclamation basis & Cf. Acts 16:14b & Philippi: Lydia attends to Paul's message; the acclamation adapts the narrative. & \chronodate{acclamation}{\chronologyannotation{acclamation}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{\textit{Narrated event:} the mission setting and Acts' composition are distinct relations; the mission dates remain disputed alternatives.}\\\midrule
Second reading & Philippians 1:20c--24, 27a & Paul writes from imprisonment to the Philippians; Rome is the place named by one received chronology claim. & \chronodate{second-reading}{\chronologyannotation{second-reading}}\\
\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.97\linewidth}@{}}{Paul's present imprisonment frames his future alternatives of continued service and being with Christ. A hoped-for departure is not a dated narrated death.}\\
\bottomrule
